  "file": "s08-hawaii-1971.md",
  "text": "In nineteen seventy-one, a team at the University of Hawaii, led by Norman Abramson, faced exactly this problem. They wanted to connect computer terminals spread across the islands to one central computer in Honolulu. Phone lines were expensive and unreliable, so they used radio. Every terminal talked to the hub on the same frequency. They called it Aloha-net.",
